<article class="card section">
  <h1>Designers</h1>
  <p class="lede">Stories of people whose work made reading clearer and design kinder.</p>

  <section class="section designers-list">
    <article class="card person">
      <div class="avatar" role="img" aria-label="Adrian Frutiger portrait placeholder"></div>
      <h2>Adrian Frutiger</h2>
      <p>Frutiger taught us that letters should be legible at a glance and comfortable across contexts. His calm, practical solutions remind designers that clarity comes from subtle, considered choices.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card person">
      <div class="avatar" role="img" aria-label="Erik Spiekermann portrait placeholder"></div>
      <h2>Erik Spiekermann</h2>
      <p>Erik bridged the craft of type with the needs of real systems. He shows how typographic care improves everyday reading — from signs to screens — by thinking about people first.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card person">
      <div class="avatar" role="img" aria-label="Matthew Carter portrait placeholder"></div>
      <h2>Matthew Carter</h2>
      <p>Matthew Carter’s work travels well between sizes and media. His letterforms give readers quiet guidance, helping words sit comfortably on the page and in the eye.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card person">
      <div class="avatar" role="img" aria-label="Edward Tufte portrait placeholder"></div>
      <h2>Edward Tufte</h2>
      <p>Tufte’s devotion to clarity and restraint is a touchstone: remove decoration that does not serve understanding, and let the content speak plainly.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</article>
